 The USB-C output adds a second regulation stage. The interface and configuration determine the voltage offered to the connected device, called the sink, and how that voltage is negotiated. Enough energy must be supplied by the upstream converter to support the selected output. A load change therefore reaches the resonant converter through the output stage and secondary storage.
 
-The isolated resonant stage in the prototype feeds the secondary bulk bus: the rectified transformer-output supply before the USB-C regulator. The MPQ4242-based output section then supplies VBUS from that bus, and fixed settings of \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} were tested. The bulk-voltage sensing input, identified as PA1 on the STM32 microcontroller, provides the feedback used to control this upstream supply. The connector-voltage measurement in the results tables is downstream of that control loop.
+The isolated resonant stage in the prototype feeds the secondary bulk bus: the rectified transformer-output supply before the USB-C regulator. The MPQ4242-based output section then supplies VBUS from that bus, and fixed settings of \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} were tested. The bulk-voltage sensing input, identified as PA1 on the MCU, provides the feedback used to control this upstream supply. The connector-voltage measurement in the results tables is downstream of that control loop.
 
 On attachment, the load can draw down secondary storage before the burst controller increases transfer. On removal, energy already in the tank can continue charging the bulk capacitor. At no load, even small repeated bursts can raise the voltage if they deliver more energy than the auxiliary circuits and leakage consume. The startup, no-load, and overvoltage states were developed to handle these conditions.
 
