 \section{ADC Sampling and Burst Timing}
 \label{sec:ctrl-architecture}
 
-The Arduino STM32 software framework was used with direct configuration of the STM32G071 peripherals. A peripheral is a hardware block inside the MCU, such as a timer, converter, or serial interface. Timer TIM1 was configured to generate the paired carrier commands on outputs PA8/PA9 in center-aligned mode, where the counter runs up and down. The resulting carrier is \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, with a nominal \SI{40}{\nano\second} interval at both transitions. A second hardware timer, TIM3, schedules the slower burst-enable envelope on output PA10. The software checks for TIM1 counter-update events for a limited interval before changing enable. This bounded polling attempts to align burst transitions with the carrier; the wait can end before an update is observed. An allowed static \SI{100}{\percent} command holds the enable output PA10 at logic high, the voltage state that permits switching, without repeated short gaps. Logic low inhibits switching; it does not mean that the power-circuit capacitors have discharged.
+The Arduino STM32 software framework was used with direct configuration of the MCU peripherals. A peripheral is a hardware block inside the MCU, such as a timer, converter, or serial interface. Timer TIM1 was configured to generate the paired carrier commands on outputs PA8/PA9 in center-aligned mode, where the counter runs up and down. The resulting carrier is \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, with a nominal \SI{40}{\nano\second} interval at both transitions. A second hardware timer, TIM3, schedules the slower burst-enable envelope on output PA10. The software checks for TIM1 counter-update events for a limited interval before changing enable. This bounded polling attempts to align burst transitions with the carrier; the wait can end before an update is observed. An allowed static \SI{100}{\percent} command holds the enable output PA10 at logic high, the voltage state that permits switching, without repeated short gaps. Logic low inhibits switching; it does not mean that the power-circuit capacitors have discharged.
 
 Analog-to-digital converter (ADC) acquisition runs independently of the foreground control loop, the processor's repeated sequence of sensing and control decisions. ADC1 samples the primary-voltage input PA0, secondary-voltage input PA1, and current-related input PA2 in sequence. DMA transfers these readings into a circular buffer, a memory area reused from its beginning when the end is reached. General-purpose input/output (GPIO) refers to the digital pin hardware used to drive an output such as enable. The universal asynchronous receiver-transmitter (UART) is the serial interface used for commands and status messages. The latest values are read, converted, and filtered on each control pass. The operating state and faults are then checked before a duty request is sent to the scheduler. The scan is free-running: the channels are sampled sequentially, without locking their phase to the carrier.
 
